================================================================================================
v106  QPE IS BLIND TO THE TWIRL:  conjugation is isospectral
================================================================================================
  N=3 crosstalk, M=9 terms, T=0.25

================================================================================================
PART 1  THE SPECTRUM DOES NOT MOVE  (tier C: operator structure, no evolution)
================================================================================================
  all 64 register values (a,b), each giving a distinct sign pattern sigma:

     (a,b)              sigma (first 6)          max |eigval - eigval(sigma=1)|
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   000000   [1,1,1,1,1,1]      0.000e+00
   000001   [ 1,-1,-1, 1, 1, 1]      0.000e+00
   000010   [ 1,-1,-1, 1,-1,-1]      1.443e-15
   000011   [ 1, 1, 1, 1,-1,-1]      1.443e-15
   000100   [ 1, 1, 1, 1,-1,-1]      1.443e-15
   000101   [ 1,-1,-1, 1,-1,-1]      1.443e-15
   ...

  WORST spectral deviation over all 64 twirls: 1.443e-15
  distinct sign patterns realised: 32

  The spectrum is INVARIANT. Any phase-based readout - QPE, and every
  eigenvalue-estimation variant of it - has exactly zero signal to decode,
  at any T, any depth, any precision. This is not a cost, it is a
  structural impossibility.

================================================================================================
PART 2  WHERE THE SIGNAL ACTUALLY LIVES  (tier B: exact amplitudes, no sampling)
================================================================================================
  Degree-1 Walsh coefficient in sigma_k of three candidate readouts, on the
  same probe and the same evolution. Only a readout with a nonzero degree-1
  coefficient can be decoded by the design register.

   readout                     max |degree-1 Walsh coeff|     usable?
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   QPE phase (spectral)             2.082e-16                 no, exactly
   return probability               3.145e-03                 weakly - see PART 3
   observable <O> after evolution   1.266e-02                 YES

   for scale, the predicted first-order size T*|c| is ~4.079e-02

================================================================================================
PART 3  A CORRECTION TO twirl_cal's DOCSTRING  (tier B: exact amplitudes)
================================================================================================
  twirl_cal says the return probability 'has NO first-order term in sigma -
  the linear part is purely imaginary and squares away'. PART 2 measures
  3.1e-03, not zero, so the statement is imprecise. Working out what it
  should say:

    order T^1 : -iT<H_sigma> is imaginary; |1-iTx|^2 = 1+T^2x^2, no T^1 term.
    order T^2 : both <H_sigma>^2 and <H_sigma^2> carry only sigma_j sigma_k
                (degree 2) and sigma_k^2 = 1 (degree 0). NO degree 1.
    order T^3 : sigma_i sigma_j sigma_k with two indices coinciding collapses
                to a SINGLE sigma. Degree 1 appears here, and not before.

  So the return probability does carry degree-1 signal - suppressed by T^2
  relative to the observable's, which is O(T). The prediction is a slope of
  3 against the observable's 1. Measured:

      T        deg-1 <O>     deg-1 P_return      ratio
   ------------------------------------------------------------------
    0.05    2.5613e-03     1.2730e-04      0.0497
    0.10    5.1150e-03     5.0846e-04      0.0994
    0.20    1.0169e-02     2.0218e-03      0.1988
    0.40    2.5164e-02     7.8970e-03      0.3138

   fitted slope d log(coeff) / d log T :   <O> 1.088      P_return 1.986
   predicted                           :   <O> 1.000     P_return 3.000

  MEASURED SLOPE IS 2, NOT 3, so the order-T^3 story above is WRONG. The
  T^2 term does reach degree 1, and PART 4 finds the mechanism.

================================================================================================
PART 4  THE DESIGN IS CONFOUNDED, AND THE COLUMNS ARE NOT FREE TO FIX
================================================================================================
  sigma_j sigma_k is itself a Walsh character: sigma_j(a,b) sigma_k(a,b) =
  (-1)^(a.(z_j+z_k) + b.(x_j+x_k)), i.e. the character of the symplectic
  vector v_j + v_k. So if v_j + v_k = v_m for some term m already in the set,
  a DEGREE-2 effect is indistinguishable from the DEGREE-1 effect of term m.
  That is classical design confounding, and here the columns are handed over
  by the Paulis - they cannot be re-chosen the way _design_spec chooses the
  QLTO register's columns.

  aliasing triples v_i + v_j = v_k found: 12

     P_i          P_j          =  P_k         c_k
   ----------------------------------------------------------------
   ZZI          XXI          =  YYI         +0.2551
   ZZI          ZII          =  IZI         +0.1285
   IZZ          IXX          =  IYY         +0.2747
   IZZ          IZI          =  IIZ         +0.1188

  RANK CHECK (tier C: operator structure).
    M = 9 distinct symplectic vectors, GF(2) dimension 2N = 6
    so at most 6 can be independent and M > 2N forces dependencies.

  WHAT THIS MEANS, and it is a real correction to the record.

    v101 proved FULL RANK of the design matrix - distinct Paulis give distinct
    symplectic vectors, distinct characters are orthogonal, so the M degree-1
    columns are independent. That theorem is correct and is NOT what is at
    stake here. Independence of the degree-1 columns says nothing about
    whether a degree-2 product COINCIDES with one of them, and for the
    XX/YY/ZZ triple on every bond it does: v_XX + v_YY = v_ZZ exactly.

    twirl_cal's commit explains its T^2 bias by saying 'even orders carry
    sigma_j sigma_k and project onto degree 2'. For a term set closed under
    such products that is false - the even order lands squarely on degree 1,
    which is why the slope above is 2. The Richardson fix still WORKS, since
    it cancels any T^2 term whatever its origin, but its stated justification
    does not hold and the bias it removes is aliasing, not truncation.

    This is the same failure the QLTO register has as resolution III, which
    v90 measured costing the Hamiltonian-learning cosine 0.714 at M=16. There
    the cure was more design rows. Here there is no such lever: the columns
    ARE the Paulis. The available cures are different - drop one term of each
    triple and infer it, or choose probes/observables making the aliased
    contribution vanish - and none of them is tested.

================================================================================================
READING IT
================================================================================================
  QPE cannot be mimicked here, and the reason is not depth. V5's QPE path
  died on depth - survival 0.098 at Heisenberg N=6 - which is a cost and
  could in principle be engineered away. This is different: the twirl is a
  similarity transformation, so a spectral readout is CONSTANT in the very
  variable the design register decodes.

  The contrast with V5 is exact and worth keeping. V5's register changed the
  STATE, so energies moved and a phase carried the signal. This register
  changes the HAMILTONIAN BY CONJUGATION, so energies do not move and only a
  fixed-basis expectation value carries it.

  Which means the O(1) already reached in v105 - 2 bases times n_probes, flat
  in N and M - is not a way station toward a phase-based version. It is the
  right answer for this construction, and the factor of 2 it keeps is a
  commutation requirement, not an inefficiency.
